Errno: find the target's errno.h when cross-compiling without a sysroot

Errno_pm.PL runs under miniperl, a host binary, so $^O is the build
machine's.  On a linux build host it takes the linux branch and reads
"$sysroot/usr/include/errno.h"; with no sysroot configured that is the
host's glibc header.  Preprocessed by the target compiler it fails
("features.h: No such file or directory"), Errno.pm comes out empty, and
the build ends up without errno constants -- or, with a compatible host
header, with the host's values.

When cross-compiling without a sysroot, ask the target compiler which
errno.h it actually includes (-M) and use that.

--- a/ext/Errno/Errno_pm.PL	2026-09-13 01:52:11.030477518 -0500
+++ b/ext/Errno/Errno_pm.PL	2026-09-13 01:52:11.313235117 -0500
@@ -112,7 +112,13 @@
     # When cross-compiling we may store a path for gcc's "sysroot" option:
     my $sysroot = $Config{sysroot} || '';
     my $linux_errno_h;
-    if ($^O eq 'linux') {
+    if ($^O eq 'linux' && $Config{usecrosscompile} && !$sysroot) {
+	# Cross-compiling without a sysroot: $^O is the build host's (miniperl
+	# runs there), and "/usr/include/errno.h" is the host's own header, not
+	# the target's.  Ask the target compiler which errno.h it includes.
+	my $dep = `echo "#include <errno.h>" | $Config{cc} $Config{ccflags} -E -M - 2>/dev/null`;
+	($linux_errno_h) = grep { -e $_ } $dep =~ /(\S*\berrno\.h)\b/g;
+    } elsif ($^O eq 'linux') {
 	# Some Linuxes have weird errno.hs which generate
 	# no #file or #line directives
 	($linux_errno_h) = grep { -e $_ } map { "$_/errno.h" }
